        <div id="capacity" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Capacity and Frame Steel
          </h2>

          <p>
            Bench capacity ratings cover the combined weight of the lifter and the load, and they vary widely: from 300 lb each for user and lifted weight in the BowFlex 5.1S manual, to 600 lb on Titan's current FID spec table, to 1,000 lb on REP's competition benches. A 200 lb lifter pressing 225 lb is putting about 425 lb on the bench before any dynamic force, so ratings in the 600 lb class are fine for most home lifters and the 1,000 lb class gives real headroom.
          </p>

          <p>
            Frame construction tells you more than the rating. Benches built from 3 by 3 inch 11-gauge steel, like the REP FB-5000 and Rogue 3.0, weigh 60 to 125 lb and barely move under load. Folding benches with thinner tubing and a wide range of adjustment points weigh under 30 lb and can feel lively even when they are nowhere near their rated limit.
          </p>

          <p>
            Treat unusually high ratings on very light benches with caution. The rating may describe the frame's breaking point in a static test, but stability under a moving load depends on footprint, weight and how tight the adjustment points are. If you plan to bench heavy, pick a bench that weighs at least 60 lb or more.
          </p>

          <p>
            When a brand publishes no rating at all, as with the Rogue 3.0, look at the frame specification and reviewers' measurements, and do not assume a number the manufacturer has not stated.
          </p>

          <p>
            One more practical point: a heavier bench is harder to move, which is a real cost if it has to be cleared away after every session. Wheels and a handle at one end solve most of that, so check for them if your bench will not live in a dedicated space.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="pad-gap" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Pad Gap, Width and Height
          </h2>

          <p>
            On an adjustable bench, the gap between the seat and the back pad is the detail you will notice most. A wide gap leaves a hollow under your lower back when the bench is flat, which can feel uncomfortable on presses and hip thrusts. Reviewers measure about 2.1 inches on the REP AB-3000 2.0, which most lifters find acceptable; some adjustable benches have larger gaps, and flat benches have none.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pad width affects stability and shoulder comfort. A 12 inch pad is standard and lets your shoulder blades move freely; a 14 inch pad, like the wide option on the REP FB-5000, gives broader lifters more support on heavy presses. Pad thickness and density matter too: a 4 inch high-density pad holds its shape, while thin, soft pads compress and let you feel the frame.
          </p>

          <p>
            Bench height matters for leg drive. Competition benches sit around 17 inches high, and REP lists the FB-5000 at 16.9 inches. If a bench is too tall for your legs, you cannot plant your feet firmly; reviewers note that some folding benches sit around 18 inches, which can be awkward for shorter lifters.
          </p>

          <p>
            Grip and cover material affect how well you stay in place. Grippy vinyl keeps your back from sliding on incline presses, while slick covers can let you drift. It is a small detail, but it shows up on every set.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="positions" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Positions You Will Actually Use
          </h2>

          <p>
            Bench marketing often leads with the number of positions, but most lifters use only a few. Flat, a low incline of around 15 to 30 degrees, a steeper incline around 45 degrees and near-vertical for seated presses cover almost every exercise. Decline is useful for some pressing and core work but optional for most people.
          </p>

          <p>
            What matters more is how quickly and securely the bench adjusts. Ladder systems like REP's let you change angles in seconds and show you exactly which setting you are using. Pop-pin systems can be just as secure but slower. Whatever the mechanism, it should lock without play; a back pad that shifts under load is distracting at best and unsafe at worst.
          </p>

          <p>
            Seat adjustment matters most at steep inclines. Without it, you slide down the bench on seated presses. Look for at least two or three seat angles if you plan to do incline work regularly.
          </p>

          <p>
            For decline work, leg rollers or a foot hold are essential. The REP AB-3000 2.0 has built-in rollers, the Titan uses foot rollers, and the BowFlex and FLYBIRD include decline settings with their own leg supports. Benches without rollers cannot be used safely in decline.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you share the bench with a partner of a different height, adjustability matters more. Seat angle, pad position relative to the rack and the height of the leg rollers all change how the bench fits each person, and a bench that adjusts quickly makes switching between lifters painless. Try to note the settings each person uses so you are not guessing every session.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="space" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Space and Storage
          </h2>

          <p>
            A weight bench takes up about 4 to 6 feet of length and 2 feet of width, plus the space you need to get on and off and handle weights around it. Measure your room with that working space included, not just the bench footprint.
          </p>

          <p>
            If the bench has to share a room, storage features become important. The BowFlex 5.1S stands upright in about 24 by 28 inches of floor, the Rogue 3.0 has a built-in vertical storage stand, and FLYBIRD's folding benches collapse to roughly 30 inches long. Wheels and a front handle make it much easier to move a heavy bench out of the way.
          </p>

          <p>
            Flooring matters too. A bench on a hard, level floor with rubber matting underneath is more stable and protects the floor from dropped dumbbells. Our guides to <a href="/best-garage-floor-mat/">garage floor mats</a> and <a href="/best-garage-floor-tiles/">garage floor tiles</a> cover rubber options that suit a garage gym.
          </p>

          <p>
            Garage gyms bring their own problems in winter. Vinyl pads can stiffen and crack in cold, and condensation can rust frames. A small <a href="/best-garage-heater/">garage heater</a> makes winter training more comfortable and helps protect equipment.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Safety and Setup
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most bench injuries come from setup rather than equipment failure. Place the bench on a level surface, check that every adjustment pin or ladder is fully engaged before loading it, and make sure the bench does not rock. A bench that wobbles empty will wobble more under load.
          </p>

          <p>
            Heavy barbell pressing should be done in a rack with safety arms or spotter bars set just below your chest, or with a spotter. Pressing heavy without either is the most common way home lifters get hurt.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check your bench periodically. Tighten bolts that loosen over time, look for cracks in welds, and inspect upholstery for tears that can catch skin. Replace worn adjustment pins and wheels rather than improvising.
          </p>

          <p>
            Respect the manufacturer's limits. If a manual, like BowFlex's, lists separate limits for user weight and lifted weight, stay within both. Ratings exist for a reason, and exceeding them can void the warranty as well as risk injury.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="care" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Care and Lifespan
          </h2>

          <p>
            A well-made steel bench can last decades with minimal care. Wipe down the pad after each session to remove sweat, which breaks down vinyl over time, and keep it out of direct sun if possible.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep adjustment mechanisms clean and lightly lubricated if the manufacturer recommends it. Ladders and pop pins collect dust and chalk, which can make adjustments stiff.
          </p>

          <p>
            Watch the wheels and feet. Rubber feet protect your floor and stop the bench from sliding; replace them if they wear through. Wheels on heavy benches take a lot of abuse and are usually cheap to replace.
          </p>

          <p>
            When a pad wears out, most major brands sell replacement pads, which is far cheaper than a new bench. That availability is one of the best reasons to buy from an established brand.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you train in a garage, keep an eye on rust. Chalk, sweat and humidity all speed it up, especially on bare steel adjustment parts. A quick wipe with a dry cloth after training, and an occasional light coat of oil on exposed steel, keeps the frame looking and working like new for years.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying on Position Count</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Thirty configurations sounds impressive, but most lifters use four or five angles. Choose on stability, pad gap and how securely the bench locks, not on how many settings it has.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting High Ratings on Light Benches</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                An 880 lb rating on a 28 lb folding bench is not the same as a 1,000 lb rating on a 125 lb steel frame. For heavy pressing, choose a heavier bench with thicker steel.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring Bench Height</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A bench that is too tall stops you from planting your feet and using leg drive. Check the height against your leg length, especially if you are shorter.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Pressing Heavy Without Safeties</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most bench injuries happen when a lifter fails a rep alone. Press heavy in a rack with safety arms set just below your chest, or with a spotter.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Manual's Limits</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Some benches list separate user and lifted weight limits. Stay within both, and check whether exceeding them affects the warranty.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

