        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best weight bench for a home gym?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most home gyms we recommend the REP Fitness AB-3000 2.0, which REP lists with eight back angles from -12 to 85 degrees and five seat angles, plus built-in decline rollers and a 1,000 lb rating per reviewers. If you only press flat and heavy, a competition flat bench like the REP FB-5000 is more stable.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a decline bench?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not usually. Flat and incline positions cover most pressing, rowing and accessory work. Decline is useful if you like decline presses or core work, but most lifters can skip it and put the money toward a sturdier flat-incline bench.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much weight can a bench hold?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Ratings on our picks range from 300 lb each for user and lifted weight in the BowFlex 5.1S manual to 1,000 lb on REP's competition benches. The rating covers you plus the load, so add your body weight to what you lift.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is pad gap on a weight bench?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is the space between the seat and the back pad on an adjustable bench. A large gap can leave a hollow under your lower back when the bench is flat. Reviewers measure about 2.1 inches on the REP AB-3000 2.0.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How tall should a weight bench be?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Competition benches sit around 17 inches high, and REP lists the FB-5000 at 16.9 inches. You should be able to plant your feet flat with your knees bent; shorter lifters may find taller benches awkward.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a folding weight bench worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For dumbbell work in a small space, yes. Folding benches are light and easy to store, but they are less stable than heavy steel frames, so they are not the best choice for heavy barbell pressing.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does a weight bench last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A heavy steel bench from an established brand can last decades with basic care. Pads and wheels are the parts that wear out, and most major brands sell replacements.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

